=== Ziph · Psalm 54
--- In the wilderness of Ziph
kicker: 1 Samuel 23 · Ziph
p: Psalm 54 is another prayerful song that was penned by David during his flight from Saul.
scripture: 1 Samuel 23:14-15

--- Jonathan strengthened his hand in God
kicker: 1 Samuel 23 · Ziph
p: Shortly after making his refuge in the woods of Ziph, Saul's son Jonathan comes to David and assures his beloved friend that his father will not find him.
scripture: 1 Samuel 23:16-18

--- Doth not David hide himself with us?
kicker: 1 Samuel 23 · Ziph
scripture: Psalm 54:1-2 | mark: Save me; Hear my prayer
p: Credited as a Psalm of David, when the Ziphims came and said to Saul, Doth not David hide himself with us? We can sense the urgency of David's prayer to God, with the plaintive Save me, and Hear my prayer.
scripture: 1 Samuel 23:19-21

--- Behold, God is my helper
kicker: Psalm 54 · Ziph
scripture: Psalm 54:3-4 | mark: strangers are risen up against me; Behold, God is mine helper
p: There are times in our life when the relative safety we have is suddenly threatened. When even strangers rise up against us, and we have unexpected enemies that we weren't even aware of...when in desperation we should turn to God.
p: Behold, God is my helper. This simple truth is reassuring in our time of trouble.

--- Them that uphold my soul
kicker: Psalm 54 · Ziph
p: And blessing on those who have our backs, who give us comfort and support, should be in our prayers as well. David says, the Lord is with them that uphold my soul.
p: In the Psalm, we see David praising those that help him, (mirroring King Saul's blessing upon the Ziphites for revealing David's hiding spot. The irony here is interesting. Especially given the context). The Ziphites and Saul pursue David, but he makes his escape to Maon. [[1 Samuel 23:24-28]]

--- He shall reward evil unto mine enemies
kicker: Psalm 54 · Ziph
big: Psalm 54:5
p: This sudden threat rising against the future King, God's anointed, troubles him enough to bring him to call for God's mercy, to deliver him with His strength, to reward his enemies for the evil they sought to do to him. To bless those that stand with him.

--- God delivers us from all trouble
kicker: Psalm 54 · Ziph
scripture: Psalm 54:6-7 | mark: he hath delivered me out of all trouble
p: What a model for believers to have, so when our fear rises, we have the assurance of this Psalm to guide us in prayer, along with the results; God delivers us from all trouble, He takes vengeance on our enemies for us. All that we may give praise to His holy name. Amen

=== The cave · Psalm 57
--- Psalm 57 · The cave at Engedi [title]
heading: Psalm 57 · The cave at Engedi

--- When he fled from Saul in the cave
kicker: Psalm 57 · The cave
big: Psalm 57:1 | mark: Be merciful unto me, O God, be merciful unto me
p: **Psalm 57** is yet another song written by David during his flight from King Saul, when he fled from Saul in the cave.
p: The psalmist cries out for mercy, twice pleading to God to bestow favor on him. Not grace, unmerited favor, but mercy because David has trusted in Him, making his refuge in the Lord.

--- Mercy and grace
kicker: Psalm 57 · The cave
p: Mercy is often said to be not receiving what you deserve, while grace is receiving what you don't deserve. God shows His mercy on us that we are not destroyed for our wickedness. Grace is then receiving eternal life and made partakers of the inheritance of Christ.
p: But asking for mercy can also mean seeking right judgment based on mitigating circumstances and motivation of the heart.

--- Mercy may be graceful
kicker: Psalm 57 · The cave
p: Grace cannot be earned and is given out of the love of the one granting it, be it man or God. Showing merciful favor, above and beyond what's expected given the facts of the matter, because the one granting it simply can.
p: Mercy may be graceful, but it may also be an understanding that other factors may be at play, that influence the judge in his judgement.

--- The mercy of the court
kicker: Psalm 57 · The cave
big: Psalm 57:2
p: David confesses his trust in God to shield himself from the calamities that he is facing, and asks for this sake that God performs this ask, the same way David trusts the Lord for all things.
p: Note David isn't boasting. He isn't telling God that he deserves anything. He is simply reminding God, and himself, that his trust is entirely placed on Him. He is throwing himself at the mercy of the court. The mighty Judge.

--- The substance of things hoped for
kicker: Psalm 57 · The cave
big: Psalm 57:3 | mark: God shall send forth his mercy and his truth
p: He then gives the sure hope he has, having yet to be rescued or brought out of calamity, seeing the thing far off, that God shall send forth His mercy and truth.
scripture: Hebrews 11:1-2

--- Saved by hope
kicker: Psalm 57 · The cave
p: The Bible tells us that hope isn't a wish, or a desire for an outcome, but the thing itself we are placing our trust in, sight unseen.
scripture: Romans 8:24-25

--- Be thou exalted, O God
kicker: Psalm 57 · The cave
p: It is this good report of the faith of David, that we are taught the value of hope. David's constant faith resulting in deliverance gives witness to us the faithfulness of God.
big: Psalm 57:4 | mark: My soul is among lions
p: Now David, in the cave at Engedi, camped with his mighty men among the mountain lions in their dens, calls out to the Lord and gives Him praise.
big: Psalm 57:5

--- How can we not when we pray?
kicker: Psalm 57 · The cave
p: Even in desperation, in danger, pleading his case to the God he's trusted, David gives Him praise.
p: How can we not when we pray?

--- Come boldly unto the throne of grace
kicker: Psalm 57 · The cave
p: We have all been guilty of haphazard prayer. Of casting our cares upon the Lord in a manner that almost seems like we are taking for granted the audience we are given to the Almighty God.
p: Yes, in Christ, we may enter boldly;
scripture: Hebrews 4:15-16

--- The High Priest
kicker: Psalm 57 · The cave
p: We need not an invitation or a human agent acting as priests, between the Lord and man. We have *the* High Priest and advocate with the Father, the holy Son of God.
p: Because our sins are remembered no more, washed clean in His blood, no other sacrifice is needed to purify ourselves before seeking an audience with God.
big: Hebrews 10:19

--- God understands
kicker: Psalm 57 · The cave
p: We can know He will hear our plea.
p: And sometimes that plea may be unintelligible to even ourselves, made in such sadness and heartache we can't find words enough to convey our fears. God understands.
scripture: Romans 8:26-27

--- Ye ask amiss
kicker: Psalm 57 · The cave
p: God makes allowances for our infirmities of the flesh. But, we may also pray amiss;
big: James 4:3

--- Self serving at best
kicker: Psalm 57 · The cave
p: We may cavalierly ask for the things we covet, offhand requests that are self serving at best, and attempts to mask sin with Godliness, at worst.
p: And we may come before the throne of grace to ask of Him that giveth generously, in an offhand way that fails to acknowledge Who He is.

--- The glory of God in view
kicker: Psalm 57 · The cave
p: David always keeps the glory of God in view during his prayers. Exalting His holy name. Humbling himself before the throne. The judgment seat that he has brought his troubles to, seeking mercy.
scripture: James 4:6-10 | mark: Humble yourselves in the sight of the Lord

--- When we pray, humble yourself
kicker: Psalm 57 · The cave
p: When we pray, humble yourself. When we are in comfort and under the hedge of protection, and blessed beyond measure, far from the dens of lions, with no man seeking our soul to destroy it, we often forget this aspect of prayer.
p: We may toss out quick and perfunctory words, asking God to bless this or that, or even to help others in need, but forgetting Who it is we have an audience with.

--- A man after His own heart
kicker: Psalm 57 · The cave
p: It may be easier in desperate times to remember our place, but it shouldn't be forgotten just because you're in His hand. Never take for granted the favor we have, the grace that's been shown, and the awesome power of God.
p: When we look at David, especially his behavior towards Saul during this time, especially this incident, and we get the context of his prayerful posture during this time of trial, and great faith towards his Redeemer to judge righteous judgment, and deliver him from evil, we can understand why God considered David a man after His own heart.

--- But we see Jesus
kicker: Psalm 57 · The cave
p: All these things are written for our admonition, here at the end of the world. David looking forward through the Tabernacle and the Temple, and the Holy Of Holies covered by a veil, has placed his trust in the continuing blessings of God.
p: But we see Jesus, we can know His righteous name. The veil is torn, the access has been granted, sanctified by the Spirit, a High Priest forever. We have a greater testimony in the Spirit and a more sure word of prophecy in the complete scripture.

--- And so we have no excuse
kicker: Psalm 57 · The cave
p: We can know the outcome of David's time in the cave, the way his humble prayer led to his conviction that if he obeyed God, and trusted in the Lord, things would turn out well.
p: Again and again through Psalms we see the same pattern play out. Throughout scripture we see miracles that move men to do great things for the Lord.

=== David spares Saul · 1 Samuel 24
--- In the wilderness of Engedi
kicker: 1 Samuel 24 · The cave
p: And so we have no excuse.
scripture: 1 Samuel 24:1-3

--- Saul's skirt
kicker: 1 Samuel 24 · The cave
scripture: 1 Samuel 24:4-6 | mark: The LORD forbid that I should do this thing unto my master

--- Among lions
kicker: 1 Samuel 24 · The cave
big: Psalm 57:6 | mark: They have prepared a net for my steps
p: This is the context of Psalm 57. David is among lions, physically and metaphorically. The mighty men with him, armed and dangerous. Encouraging David to kill Saul.
p: Saul, who is irrationally envious of David, and seeking his life has prepared a net for the younger man's steps. The King has happened upon the caves, digging a pit to capture David, but Saul himself has fallen into it. The hunter is in the snare of the hunted.

--- David does the right thing
kicker: 1 Samuel 24 · The cave
p: Defenseless. Caught with his pants down, quite literally. David has the opportunity to take matters into his own hands, to be his own avenger. To slay the King and let whatever may come of it unfold.
p: And instead, David trusts in the Lord and does the right thing.

--- Mine eye spared thee
kicker: 1 Samuel 24 · The cave
p: He cuts the piece of robe, silently. Even this act of rebellion pricks his heart. He has done nothing wrong to deserve what's happening, he has trusted in God, blameless before Him. Even this slight move against the King makes David humble before God. His trust and meekness is rewarded.
p: Following Saul out of the cave, at a distance, he cries out to his oppressor;
big: 1 Samuel 24:10

--- See the skirt of thy robe
kicker: 1 Samuel 24 · The cave
p: He treats his enemy to a sign of peace. David makes known to Saul and his armies exactly the heart he has towards his King.
big: 1 Samuel 24:11

--- The true Judge
kicker: 1 Samuel 24 · The cave
p: David then places the entire matter before the true Judge;
big: 1 Samuel 24:12

--- And Saul wept
kicker: 1 Samuel 24 · The cave
p: Reminding Saul of his exaggerated hatred for David, a humble flea, pleading his innocence, and appealing to God, David has negotiated the way of peace, and left it to the Lord to decide his fate. And Saul weeps.
scripture: 1 Samuel 24:15-17 | mark: Thou art more righteous than I

--- Thou shalt surely be king
kicker: 1 Samuel 24 · The cave
p: Saul relents, admits (for the time being) that he was wrong. That David sparing his life shows the righteousness of the man before God.
scripture: 1 Samuel 24:19-20

--- He gives all credit to God
kicker: 1 Samuel 24 · The cave
p: The King asks only mercy for his sons when David comes into his power, and then rides away. Defeated by the truth and the faith of David, who did the right thing, and left his fate to the Lord.

--- I will sing unto thee among the nations
kicker: Psalm 57 · The cave
scripture: Psalm 57:7-11 | mark: I will sing unto thee among the nations
p: And for this, David didn't praise himself. He didn't pat himself on the back for having done the right thing. He gives all credit to God. He sings unto Him among the nations. He exalts God above the heavens and His glory above the earth.

--- We give all honor to God
kicker: Psalm 57 · The cave
p: We as Christians today may find ourselves on our knees in times of crisis, begging to God to save us. But how often does He deliver us and we then credit ourselves? How many times do we forget his tender mercies? How often do we turn in praise to the congregation, letting them know the power of God in your life?
p: We don't have to write an entire songbook like David, praising God's holy name. But we should take the opportunity whenever possible to let others know and to let God know we appreciate what has been done in our lives.
center: That even when we do the right thing and make that decision like David, we don't boast of ourselves. We give all honor to God. Amen

